  <div class="label mb-3">How We Operate</div>
  <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl md:text-5xl font-semibold leading-[1.1] mb-6">Editorial Standards</h1>
  <p class="text-lg text-2 mb-10">
    These are the rules we follow. They apply to every piece of content on ACESLIVEPH — articles,
    reviews, rankings, newsletter issues, social posts, video content. They exist because trust takes
    years to build and one careless post to break.
  </p>

  <h2>Our voice</h2>
  <p>
    ACESLIVEPH is the friend who actually plays. Not the journalist who covers the game from a press box.
    Not the operator who's selling you something. The grinder who walks into the room, sits down, and tells you
    the truth about what just happened. We are player-first, plain-spoken, willing to have opinions and defend them,
    willing to call out bad rakes and shady operators, willing to praise the good ones.
  </p>

  <h2>The Three-Source Rule</h2>
  <p>
    Before publishing anything negative about an identified individual or operator, we require at minimum:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Two independent sources</strong> confirming the fact (not two people who heard it from the same place), or</li>
    <li><strong>One source plus documentary evidence</strong> (screenshots, photos, public records, official statements).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If we have only one verbal source, the story stays unpublished or gets framed in non-attributable terms
    ("multiple regs in Manila have raised concerns about the rake structure at [room]" rather than naming an individual operator without documentation).
  </p>

  <h2>Right of Reply</h2>
  <p>
    Before publishing anything substantively negative about an identified person or operator, we contact them
    and offer a chance to respond. Email or DM. We give them 24 hours.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>If they respond, we include their response in the piece.</li>
    <li>If they don't respond, we publish with a note that they were given the chance.</li>
    <li>If they threaten or try to bully us out of publishing, we publish anyway — with documentation of the threat.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Things we never publish</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Allegations of cheating against an identified individual without documentary evidence.</li>
    <li>Allegations of criminal conduct against any identified party without documentary evidence and legal review.</li>
    <li>Specific accusations of poker-room collusion or game-rigging without documentary evidence.</li>
    <li>Personal information about a player they haven't made public themselves (real name if they go by a handle, home address, family details, employer).</li>
    <li>Photos of identifiable players mid-hand without their permission.</li>
    <li>Speculation about a player's mental health, addiction, or financial trouble unless they've spoken about it publicly.</li>
    <li>Content about a player's personal life unless they've made it public themselves.</li>
    <li>Schedules or details for underground / unlicensed games.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Affiliate independence</h2>
  <p>
    ACESLIVEPH earns commissions from some of the operators we review. This does not affect our coverage:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Editorial decisions are made before any affiliate negotiation.</li>
    <li>An operator's affiliate payout structure does not influence ranking position.</li>
    <li>If an operator threatens to pull an affiliate deal because of editorial coverage, we publish the piece, end the affiliate relationship, and write a separate piece about the threat.</li>
    <li>If we wouldn't write a positive review of something unpaid, we don't write one because of the affiliate either.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    See our <a href="/affiliate">Affiliate Disclosure</a> for the full list of operators we have or are pursuing relationships with.
  </p>

  <h2>Sponsored content rules</h2>
  <p>
    When a brand pays for content placement (e.g., a Player Spotlight, a featured listing):
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Always labeled <strong class="orange">"Sponsored"</strong> at the top of the post.</li>
    <li>The sponsor reviews factual accuracy of statements about themselves; they do not have editorial control over framing.</li>
    <li>We do not accept money to write a positive review of something we wouldn't have recommended unpaid.</li>
    <li>If the sponsor pulls the deal because we won't change our framing, we publish anyway and refund the sponsorship.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Corrections and retractions</h2>
  <p>We will get things wrong. The rule is: own it fast, own it visibly.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Factual error:</strong> corrected within 24 hours of confirmation. An "Updated [date]: corrected [fact]" note is added at the top of the article.</li>
    <li><strong>Significant factual error or unfounded accusation:</strong> retracted publicly. The retraction is pinned to the top of the relevant article and published as a standalone post on social channels. We apologize directly to the affected party.</li>
    <li><strong>Pattern of errors from a contributor:</strong> the contributor is dropped.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A correction we own is a credibility builder. A correction we hide is a credibility killer.</p>

  <h2>AI-assisted content</h2>
  <p>
    Some ACESLIVEPH content is AI-assisted — particularly news aggregation summaries, review templates, and educational
    glossary content. Our rule:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>AI is a starting draft, never the final published piece.</li>
    <li>Every AI-assisted piece is reviewed and edited by a human before publishing.</li>
    <li>We do not publish raw AI output as editorial content.</li>
    <li>AI is never used to fabricate quotes, sources, or facts.</li>
    <li>AI tools used: Claude (Anthropic), GPT (OpenAI), and similar large language models. We do not list specific model versions in articles unless relevant to the topic.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Reporting an issue</h2>
  <p>
    If you spot an error, an undisclosed conflict of interest, or a factual claim you can disprove,
    contact <a href="mailto:editorial@acesliveph.com">editorial@acesliveph.com</a>. We respond to every editorial complaint
    within 48 hours. Where the complaint is valid, we correct or retract publicly.
  </p>

